mike.sales: Heads-up: ACME Pilot kicked off. They're evaluating streaming chat + function-calls and need a short integration runbook for credential rotation, structured event schema, and a cost estimate for a 2-week reserved burst. Can someone own the tech details and a one-page pricing note?

ingrid.se: I can draft the runbook. Quick clarifying Qs: are they using our JS SDK server-side or a sidecar proxy? Also do they want SSE or websocket streaming?

mike.sales: They said server-side Node (Koa) and prefer SSE initially (they have strict firewall rules). Sidecar isn't an option for them.

ingrid.se: OK. I'll post a minimal example for streaming + function-calls with credential rotation for short-lived creds. Use this pattern (Node):

```js
// simplified example using @redwood-inference/sdk (server-side streaming)
const Redwood = require('@redwood-inference/sdk');
const client = new Redwood.Client({ apiKey: process.env.RW_KEY });

// start stream
const stream = await client.stream({
  model: 'redwood-rtc-1',
  mode: 'chat',
  stream: true,
  handlers: {
    onToken: (t) => process.stdout.write(t),
    onFunctionCall: async (fn) => {
      // example tool call flow - return structured JSON
      if (fn.name === 'get_customer_profile') {
        return { id: 'acme-123', tier: 'enterprise' };
      }
    }
  }
});

// Graceful credential rotation: use short-lived tokens and fetch on demand
async function getFreshKey() {
  // call internal token service which uses KMS to sign temporary key
  const r = await fetch('https://internal/auth/short-lived');
  return r.json(); // { key: 'rw_abc', expires_at: 169... }
}

setInterval(async () => {
  const creds = await getFreshKey();
  client.updateKey(creds.key); // SDK method to swap key without restarting
}, 5 * 60 * 1000); // refresh every 5m
```

ingrid.se: For SSE vs WS: SSE is simpler and plays nicer with proxies but no bi-directional binary; websocket gives lower overhead if they need many concurrent back-and-forth tool calls. They said firewall -> stick with SSE.

sam.engineer: On structured output / validation: recommend using a small JSON schema that the model returns under `tool_result` field. Example schema for `order_event`:

```json
{
  "type": "object",
  "properties": {
    "order_id": {"type": "string"},
    "status": {"type": "string"},
    "items": {"type": "array"},
    "total_cents": {"type": "integer"}
  },
  "required": ["order_id", "status", "total_cents"]
}
```

sam.engineer: Also suggest a checksum on the payload and a small TTL for cached KV to avoid stale prefixes when they rotate creds.

dhruv.solutions: Pricing piece: for a 2-week reserved burst we can quote a temporary dedicated pool with commit for the window + overage. Ballpark: $X/hour for one GPU-equivalent pod (I'll fill exact pricing), plus streaming tokens. I need their expected tokens/sec and concurrency.

mike.sales: ACME estimates 12 QPS sustained, peak 30 QPS, avg 60 tokens per request, tool-calls add ~40 tokens/call. They want firm latency SLO: p50 < 120ms, p95 < 350ms.

dhruv.solutions: With those numbers, recommend a 2-node reserved pool (N4-equivalent). I'll model cost and post numbers by EOD tomorrow. For overage, we can permit burst via autoscale - we just need to confirm network egress policy.

elaine.security: Security questions: SOC2 status is fine. They asked about KMS key material being customer-managed (HSM). We support KMS integration and can accept CMKs for Private deployments; for hosted we provide envelope encryption and offer HSM integration via a partner. Also: FIPS endpoints? We have an isolated FIPS-compliant path in select regions — will need a legal/customer-admin approval.

elaine.security: On credential rotation: we recommend short-lived credentials minted by their identity service (STS) that our SDK accepts via `client.updateKey()`. For audit, enable token-level logs in Redwood Console and forward to their SIEM (we support S3/CloudWatch ingestion).

mike.sales: Great. Can you add a short checklist for the customer-run checklist: firewall ports, allowed egress, short-lived token issuer URL, schema, and who to contact for rapid troubleshooting?

ingrid.se: I'll assemble the runbook and include an example Koa middleware snippet for token vending + SDK key swap.

```js
// Koa token proxy (very small)
app.get('/token', async (ctx) => {
  // verify incoming session, then call internal STS to mint short key
  const key = await internalSTS.mint(ctx.state.user.id, { ttl: 10 * 60 });
  ctx.body = { key };
});
```

